  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m "Logging" is a new subsystem, not an edit to a flow that already exists here — there's
  no logger, no config, no log destination, and the two surfaces (browser and Node) have different answers for where
  logs even go. So I'll run the full process: clarifying questions, approaches, a sectioned design, then a written spec
  before any code.

  One thing I want to flag up front, because it shapes the design rather than being a detail to fix later: the code path
  you'd most want instrumented is the login flow, and it already logs [38;5;153musername[39m in plaintext. A logging subsystem that
  ships logs anywhere off the user's machine turns that into a credential/PII exposure question. I'll build redaction
  into the design rather than bolting it on.

  First question — the two surfaces are genuinely different problems:

  The browser side and the Node side don't share a runtime, a log destination, or a debugging story. Instrumenting the
  browser app means deciding where logs go when the code is running on someone else's machine (console only, or shipped
  to a remote collector) — that's a real interface and privacy decision. Instrumenting the Node side means levels and
  stderr formatting, which is much cheaper. Doing both with one shared module is possible but costs an abstraction layer
  that only pays off if both surfaces actually grow.
